---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const netCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Packets, not letters', note: 'Data is chopped into small packets, each labelled with destination. Different packets can take different routes and arrive out of order — the network never promised neatness.' },
    { math: '2 · IP — the addressing layer', note: 'Every device gets an IP address (like 142.250.4.100). Routers forward each packet hop by hop, reading only the destination — no single router knows the whole path.' },
    { math: '3 · TCP — the reliability layer', note: 'Packets get lost; TCP re-requests them, reorders them, and throttles speed when the network chokes. Your file arrives whole because TCP rebuilt it.' },
    { math: '4 · DNS — the phone book', note: 'Humans type names (google.com); the Domain Name System translates them to IPs, cached at every level for speed.' },
    { math: '5 · HTTPS — the trust layer', note: 'TLS encrypts the connection and proves the server is who it claims (certificates). The padlock icon is mathematics holding the middle of the internet shut.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/networking-how-the-internet-works">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The internet is <strong>a packet-switched network with no owner and no centre</strong>. Its
    genius is layering: each layer solves one problem and trusts the ones below — wires carry
    bits, IP routes packets, TCP makes it reliable, DNS makes it memorable, TLS makes it safe.
    Designed to survive partial failure (its original ARPANET brief in 1969), it outages
    gracefully instead of collapsing completely.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the journey of one page request, layer by layer" widget="equationStep" config={netCfg} />

  <h2>What happens in those 200 milliseconds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>DNS lookup</strong> (~20 ms, usually cached) — name to address.</li>
    <li><strong>TCP handshake</strong> (1 round trip) — "may I?" "yes" "starting".</li>
    <li><strong>TLS handshake</strong> (1–2 more round trips) — certificates exchanged, keys agreed.</li>
    <li><strong>HTTP request/response</strong> — your browser asks for HTML, receives it, then repeats for every image, style sheet and script it discovers. This is why fewer files and caching matter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The design lessons worth stealing</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>End-to-end principle:</strong> the network stays simple; smarts live at the edges
    (your devices) — which is why the internet could absorb video, voice and apps nobody
    predicted. <strong>Best-effort delivery:</strong> no guarantees inside, guarantees assembled
    at the ends. Nearly every distributed system in the expert rungs reuses both ideas.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cerf & Kahn (1974); Kurose & Ross, Computer Networking; RFC 793 (TCP), RFC 1035 (DNS).</p>
</TopicPage>
